# This repo's dependency tree has two pre-existing peer-dependency conflicts
# that predate React 19 having landed here:
#
#   1. @reduxjs/toolkit@1.9.7 declares peerOptional react "^16.9.0 || ^17.0.0 || ^18",
#      but the app runs react@19.1.0.
#   2. react-redux@9.2.0 declares peer redux "^5.0.0", but the app has redux@4.2.1.
#
# Without this setting BOTH `npm install` and `npm ci` fail with ERESOLVE, which
# means CI's install step cannot succeed and nobody can reproduce the build from
# a clean checkout. package-lock.json was already generated with legacy peer
# resolution, so this file makes the repo's real, working resolution explicit
# and reproducible rather than depending on whoever installs passing a flag.
#
# This is a stopgap, not the cure. The proper fix is upgrading @reduxjs/toolkit
# to v2, which supports React 19 and brings redux 5 — resolving both conflicts at
# once. That is a major version bump and needs the app actually run to verify, so
# it is deliberately not bundled into the design-parity work.
legacy-peer-deps=true
